  "functionalReqs": [
    "Limit requests per client per rule (user ID, IP, API key, endpoint)",
    "Return HTTP 429 with retry information when a limit is exceeded",
    "Rules are configurable without a deploy",
    "Works across a fleet of API servers"
  ],
  "hints": [
    "Where does the limiter live: in the client, inside each service, or in a gateway or middleware layer?",
    "Compare token bucket, leaking bucket, fixed window, sliding window log and sliding window counter on memory use and burst behaviour.",
    "Use counters in Redis with atomic Lua scripts or INCR+EXPIRE, rules stored in config and cached locally, and X-RateLimit-* headers. Discuss race conditions and synchronisation across data centres."
  ],
  "level": "Beginner",
  "nonFunctionalReqs": [
    "Adds under 5 ms latency per request",
    "Accurate enough that a client cannot exceed the limit by much",
    "If the limiter fails, the API keeps serving (fail open)"
  ],
  "problemStatement": "Design a **distributed rate limiter** that caps how many requests a client (user, IP or API key) can make to an API in a time window, for example 100 requests per minute.\n\nIt sits in the request path of every API call, so it has to be fast and must keep working correctly across many API servers.",
